  "estimatedMinutes": 2,
  "statement": "In a right triangle with sides \\(a\\) and \\(b,\\) and hypotenuse \\(c,\\) the altitude drawn on the hypotenuse is \\(x.\\) Then:",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(ab=x^2\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac1a+\\dfrac1b=\\dfrac1x\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(a^2+b^2=2x^2\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac1{x^2}=\\dfrac1{a^2}+\\dfrac1{b^2}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac1x=\\dfrac ba\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "d",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1956-p38.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Equating two area formulas gives \\[ \\frac12ab=\\frac12cx, \\] so \\(ab=cx.\\) Squaring and using \\(c^2=a^2+b^2\\) yields \\[ a^2b^2=x^2(a^2+b^2). \\] Dividing by \\(a^2b^2x^2\\) gives \\[ \\frac1{x^2}=\\frac1{a^2}+\\frac1{b^2}. \\]\n\nThus, the correct answer is D.",
      "hints": [
        "Compute the triangle’s area using either the legs or the hypotenuse and its altitude",
        "From \\(ab=cx,\\) substitute \\(c^2=a^2+b^2\\) and divide by \\(a^2b^2x^2\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
